      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Wrapping Legacy APIs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const read = p => new Promise((res, rej) =>",
            "  fs.readFile(p, (e, d) => {",
            "    if (e) return rej(e);",
            "    res(d);        // 2nd resolve = no-op",
            "  }));"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Idempotence kills the double-callback bug class.",
            "`Promise.resolve(v)` / `reject(e)` = already settled.",
            "`Promise.resolve(p)` on a promise returns it unchanged."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. then / catch / finally",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Same as",
            "Returns"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              ".then(f, r)",
              "—",
              "new promise"
            ],
            [
              ".catch(f)",
              ".then(undefined, f)",
              "new promise"
            ],
            [
              ".finally(f)",
              "both paths",
              "value passes through"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`.then` ALWAYS returns a new promise — that's chaining.",
            "Handlers run as microtasks, never synchronously.",
            "`Promise.resolve(1).then(log)` logs after all sync code.",
            "Returning a thenable adopts its state — flattens, not nests."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Two Promise Traps",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// anti-pattern: wrapping a promise",
            "new Promise(res => doAsync().then(res));",
            "// just:",
            "return doAsync();"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`throw` inside `.then` rejects the returned promise.",
            "So `throw e` and `reject(e)` are equivalent there.",
            "Promises are EAGER — the executor runs at construction.",
            "No built-in laziness, no built-in cancel."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Flat Chain",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "readConfig()",
            "  .then(connect)",
            "  .then(query)",
            "  .then(write)",
            "  .catch(handle);   // always terminate"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Most common chaining bug",
          "text": "Not returning the inner promise. The chain moves on before the work finishes, and errors escape the `.catch`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Chaining, Propagation, Combinators",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Errors Flow Downhill",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A rejection skips every `.then` until the first `.catch`.",
            "`.catch` returning a value RESUMES in fulfilled state.",
            "To keep failing, rethrow inside the `.catch`.",
            "Unterminated chain = unhandled rejection."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Placement",
            "Catches errors from `a`?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              ".then(a).catch(b)",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              ".then(a, b)",
              "no — upstream only"
            ],
            [
              "chain returned",
              "caller's problem"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Fork vs Sequence",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two `.then` on the SAME promise = two independent chains.",
            "That branches; it does not sequence.",
            "Passing values down needs closure nesting or an accumulator.",
            "That ergonomic weakness is what async/await removes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "c.query(sql)",
            " .then(use)",
            " .catch(log)",
            " .finally(() => c.release());  // both paths"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Combinators Open",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Settles when",
            "Order"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "all",
              "first rejection / all done",
              "INPUT order"
            ],
            [
              "allSettled",
              "all settled, never rejects",
              "INPUT order"
            ],
            [
              "race",
              "first to settle, either way",
              "n/a"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "allSettled yields {status,value} or {status,reason}.",
            "Use it when partial failure is acceptable.",
            "`race` is the basis of the classic timeout pattern."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "all fails fast, cancels nothing",
          "text": "The other operations keep running after the first rejection — and their later rejections can surface as unhandled."
        },
