    {
      "page_title": "Combinator Picks & async/await (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Stack traces",
          "text": "async/await preserves async frames far better than raw `.then` chains — `--async-stack-traces` is on by default."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Serial Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const a = await getA();   // t(A)",
            "const b = await getB();   // + t(B)  serial!",
            "const [x, y] = await Promise.all([",
            "  getA(), getB()          // max(t), not sum(t)",
            "]);"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`await` in a `for` loop is intentionally serial.",
            "Correct for rate-limited APIs and ordered writes.",
            "Disastrous for 500 independent fetches.",
            "`map(async x => f(x))` gives PROMISES, not values."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Start Early, Await Later",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const pa = getA(), pb = getB();  // both started",
            "pb.catch(() => {});   // guard the loser",
            "use(await pa, await pb);"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Creation time and await time are independent.",
            "That independence is exactly how you get parallelism.",
            "Hazard: `pa` rejects first, `pb` goes unhandled.",
            "Attach `.catch` at creation, or use `allSettled`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Forcing Sequence",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "items.reduce(",
            "  (p, x) => p.then(() => f(x)),",
            "  Promise.resolve()   // one at a time",
            ");"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Parallelism is correctness",
          "text": "Never parallelize writes that depend on ordering or share a transaction. Speed is the second question."
